# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Lecture 46 vignette pool A -- Cytopenias and Hemochromatosis (Ward): anemia indices and first approach (s6-8),
iron deficiency causes, presentation, diagnosis and replacement (s10-21), anemia of chronic disease (s23-27),
sideroblastic anemia and lead poisoning (s29-36). Every slide in the range viewed at 1920 px.

KEYS ARE WRITTEN SHORT ON PURPOSE -- detail lives in the explanation.
EXPLANATIONS follow tools/question_writing/EXPLAIN_DEEP.md: the key names the decisive clue and the slide fact;
each wrong choice says why it fails for THIS patient, the replacing fact, and a "Tell:".

Laboratory ranges: the deck gives mean corpuscular volume 80-100 fL and mean corpuscular hemoglobin 27-31 pg (s7);
the other ranges in the stems are standard adult reference ranges (hemoglobin, hematocrit, ferritin 15-200 ng/mL,
total iron-binding capacity 250-450 mcg/dL, serum iron 60-170 mcg/dL, transferrin saturation 20-50%) and only set
the scene; no key turns on a range the deck does not give, except the deck's own cut-offs (ferritin below 30 ng/mL
in chronic disease, s25; hemoglobin below 10 g/dL for erythropoietin, s26; hematocrit halfway by 3 weeks, s20).

Deliberately NOT keyed: the serum iron direction of anemia of chronic disease (s13 low vs s27 up or down; iron
panel items turn on ferritin and total iron-binding capacity, plus transferrin saturation where s13 and s32 agree);
any dose (s18, s19, s21, s33) or brand name; no blood lead number (the deck gives none, so lead stems say "mildly"
or "severely" elevated); no stem names a lead line (the gum line is described instead). Population strategies
(IO_POP) are in another pool; the one child here (hereditary sideroblastic type) is keyed on the type, not on age care.
"""
import os, sys
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
from _cmse3_q import Q
from cms_e8l46_scope import *


def S(topic, io, q, opts, cite, slot):
    d = Q(topic, io, q, opts, cite); d["slot"] = slot; return d


def V(topic, io, q, opts, cite, slot, lead):
    d = S(topic, io, q, opts, cite, slot); d["lead"] = lead; return d


def ORD(d, asc):
    d["ordered"] = True; d["asc"] = asc; return d


