# ---------------------------------------------------------------- sideroblastic anemia and lead poisoning
V(T_SIDERO, IO_A,
  "A 36-year-old man takes isoniazid for latent tuberculosis, lisinopril for hypertension, atorvastatin for high cholesterol and omeprazole for reflux. His hemoglobin is 9.5 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), and a bone marrow aspirate shows ring sideroblasts (iron-containing mitochondria forming a ring around the nucleus). Which medication should be stopped?",
  [["Isoniazid",
    "Correct. Ring sideroblasts define sideroblastic anemia, and isoniazid is one of the drugs behind its acquired, reversible type (with chlorambucil, alcohol, copper deficiency, hypothermia and lead). Treatment begins by removing the offending agent."],
   ["Omeprazole",
    "Omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor, is a medication cause of iron deficiency, not of sideroblastic anemia, which has excess body iron. Tell: ring sideroblasts point to isoniazid, not to acid suppression.", "competing diagnosis"],
   ["Lisinopril",
    "Lisinopril is not among the reversible causes of sideroblastic anemia (alcohol, isoniazid, chlorambucil, copper deficiency, hypothermia, lead poisoning). Tell: the antituberculosis drug is the offender.", "common lay belief"],
   ["Atorvastatin",
    "Atorvastatin is not a listed cause of sideroblastic anemia; the drugs named are isoniazid and chlorambucil. Tell: look for the antituberculosis drug when ring sideroblasts appear.", "common lay belief"]],
  CS(29, 32, 33), "avoid", "avoid"),

